<h2>PART 7 — Current Search</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data:</strong> local KJV text, book names, book codes.</li>
<li><strong>Library:</strong> Fuse.js for fuzzy matching.</li>
<li><strong>Ranking:</strong> exact references first, then exact phrases, then Fuse score.</li>
<li><strong>Reference search:</strong> yes, <code>parseVerseReference()</code>.</li>
<li><strong>People/places/themes:</strong> indirectly via keyword matching against <code>characters.json</code> events for question queries (who/what/where).</li>
<li><strong>Scalability:</strong> in-memory; fine for KJV, not for full multi-version corpus.</li>
<li><strong>API-backed future:</strong> would require YouVersion Search API or a RiC backend with a persistent index.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 8 — Current Notes / Bookmarks</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Storage:</strong> <code>localStorage</code> primary; <code>IndexedDB</code> secondary; <code>Firebase Firestore</code> cloud sync when signed in.</li>
<li><strong>Persistence:</strong> yes, across sessions.</li>
<li><strong>User ownership:</strong> only when Firebase-signed in; otherwise local-only.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-device sync:</strong> via Firestore when signed in.</li>
<li><strong>YouVersion connection:</strong> none.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 9 — Cross-Reference / Relationship Engine</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>bible.ts getCrossReferenceMatches()</code> computes shared non-stopword terms between verses and scores by term overlap, same book/chapter, etc.</li>
<li><code>App.tsx</code> builds a 3D/2D network: center verse, related verses, theme nodes, and a Bible hierarchy (book → chapter → verse).</li>
<li>The graph is custom WebGL (not Cytoscape, despite the dependency).</li>
<li>This is the <em>beginning</em> of a Scripture Graph, but it is a term-similarity graph, not a curated cross-reference matrix or semantic graph. It would need real cross-reference data and embeddings to become the “RiC Scripture Graph.”</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 10 — Current Geography</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data:</strong> <code>public/data/places.json</code> (~80 places) with <code>lat</code>, <code>lng</code>, descriptions, and passage ranges.</li>
<li><strong>Engine:</strong> <code>src/places.ts</code> loads, indexes, and matches places to verses.</li>
<li><strong>Map:</strong> Google Maps with custom light/dark styles in <code>mapStyles.ts</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Static vs. dynamic:</strong> static, hand-curated.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 11 — Current People / Characters</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Data:</strong> <code>public/data/characters.json</code> (~40 figures) with <code>name</code>, <code>aliases</code>, <code>era</code>, <code>approxDateRange</code>, <code>summary</code>, and <code>events</code> (each tied to <code>placeId</code> and passages).</li>
<li><strong>Engine:</strong> <code>src/characters.ts</code> loads, indexes, searches, and builds journey paths.</li>
<li>All data is hard-coded.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 12 — Current Timeline / Journey System</h2>
<ul>
<li><code>getCharacterPath()</code> sorts a character’s events and resolves their places.</li>
<li>UI in the Map tab can “play” a character’s animated journey.</li>
<li>Dates are approximate and hand-curated.</li>
<li>This could become a node in a larger Scripture/History Graph if backed by a real data pipeline.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 13 — Current PWA / Mobile App</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>PWA:</strong> <code>manifest.json</code> and icons are present; no service worker is registered in the source, so offline caching is not implemented.</li>
<li><strong>Mobile:</strong> Capacitor 8 Android project exists; it packages the web app as a WebView. No native plugins beyond the default.</li>
<li><strong>YouVersion SDK in mobile:</strong> same web SDK runs in WebView; OAuth redirects may need a Capacitor deep-link plugin.</li>
<li><strong>React Native/Expo:</strong> not used; a rewrite is not recommended.</li>
</ul>

<h2>PART 14 — Current Live Website</h2>
<p><code>https://rootedinchrist.faith/</code> serves the same built Vite SPA as the repo. A raw <code>GET</code> returns only the app shell; functionality is entirely client-side.</p>
