<h2>PART 20 — Target Architecture Flow</h2>
<pre>
CURRENT RiC
   ↓
Local KJV + YouVersion REST + hand-curated data
   ↓
WHAT ACTUALLY WORKS: KJV search/reader, local notes, visual demos
   ↓
CURRENT DATA SOURCES: public-domain KJV, curated places/characters, YVP API
   ↓
CURRENT ARCHITECTURE: Vite React SPA, browser storage, Firebase sync
   ↓
YOUTUBEERSION CAPABILITIES: auth, versions, passages, audio, search, data exchange
   ↓
OVERLAP: content, auth, search, audio
   ↓
PRESERVE: offline KJV, visual graph, geography/timeline concept
   ↓
REFACTOR: monolithic components, direct API calls, storage sync
   ↓
REPLACE: hand-curated data, rule-based insights, localStorage-only persistence
   ↓
YOUTUBEERSION PROVIDES: translations, auth, audio, search, user data exchange
   ↓
RiC MUST BUILD: Scripture Graph, canonical reference layer, geography/timeline engine, RAG study companion
   ↓
FINAL TARGET ARCHITECTURE: RiC service/adapter over YouVersion SDK/REST, graph DB, expanded datasets, clean UI modules
   ↓
DEVELOPMENT ORDER:
  1. Fix YouVersion auth
  2. Wrap YVP behind RiC Bible service
  3. Expand data + graph
  4. Add RAG/AI
</pre>

<h2>PART 21 — Final Recommendation</h2>
<p><strong>Option C — RiC abstraction layer over the official SDK/REST API.</strong></p>
<p>Use the YouVersion SDK for authentication and branded UI primitives. Use the YouVersion REST API for Bible content, search, and audio. Wrap both behind a <code>RiC BibleClient / BibleService</code> so the rest of the app never imports <code>@youversion</code> directly. This decouples RiC from the external provider, enables transparent offline KJV fallback, and makes the data layer testable and swappable.</p>

<h2>PART 22 — No Architecture Changes Made</h2>
<p>This report is baseline only. No code was modified, no data was deleted, and no credentials were exposed.</p>
